course: dictionary
lesson: 7925
title: Reactor: a machine that makes power
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A reactor is a large machine. In a nuclear reactor, atoms are split apart to make heat, and that heat is used to make electricity. «In 1986 a reactor at the Chornobyl power plant in Ukraine exploded and spread radiation.» The sentence tells you a reactor is part of a power plant.
explain: Use the words around reactor to work out its meaning. Power plant, electricity and exploded are clues. Try your meaning in the sentence: «a large machine that makes heat for electricity» still makes sense, so you have probably got it right.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Reactor can also mean any container where chemical reactions take place. «The pine trees nearest the reactor were killed by radiation» is about the big power station machine, so the clues point to the first meaning.
fact: A reactor is a large machine, often a nuclear reactor, that makes heat to produce electricity.
fact: In a nuclear reactor, atoms are split to make heat.
fact: Reactor can also mean any container where chemical reactions take place.
fact: «Radiation» means invisible harmful rays that you cannot see.
fact: Phrases you may meet are «nuclear reactor» and «reactor core».
exercise: In a power station, what does a nuclear reactor do? | *Makes heat to produce electricity | Stores books for a library | Carries people across the sea | Grows crops for the village
exercise: «The pine trees nearest the reactor were killed by radiation.» What does radiation mean here? | Sound waves from a radio | A kind of gentle weather | Bright colours in the sky | *Invisible harmful rays
exercise: Which sentence uses reactor in its second meaning? | *The students watched the liquids change inside the reactor on the lab bench | The reactor was built to hold the crew of a ship | The reactor at the power station made electricity for the town | The reactor is the biggest room in the school
test: «People had to leave a large area around the reactor.» What do the words «had to leave» suggest? | People wanted a new house nearby | People got lost on a walk | *People were forced to move away for safety | People went on holiday
test: Why do reactors need safety rules? | Because they are painted bright red | Because they make tasty food | Because they are very small | *Because accidents can release harmful radiation
exam: In «nuclear reactors in power stations make electricity without burning coal», which clue tells you a reactor is a machine for making power? | Reactors are only used in schools | Reactors are made of coal | Reactors are in power stations and make electricity | *Reactors make electricity without burning coal, which shows they use a different way to make power
